WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.476 position:50% size:92% align:center
The block begins with values one through eight.

00:00:03.476 --> 00:00:06.168 position:50% size:92% align:center
The required total is thirty-six point zero.

00:00:06.388 --> 00:00:09.427 position:50% size:92% align:center
The important GPU question is how one thread can

00:00:09.427 --> 00:00:12.212 position:50% size:92% align:center
safely use values produced by other threads.

00:00:14.232 --> 00:00:17.219 position:50% size:92% align:center
Each thread writes its input into shared memory

00:00:17.219 --> 00:00:20.269 position:50% size:92% align:center
on the SM. This workspace belongs to this block.

00:00:20.489 --> 00:00:22.921 position:50% size:92% align:center
A thread’s own working value and the block’s

00:00:22.921 --> 00:00:25.021 position:50% size:92% align:center
shared array have different ownership.

00:00:27.041 --> 00:00:29.833 position:50% size:92% align:center
All eight block threads reach the initial block

00:00:29.833 --> 00:00:32.566 position:50% size:92% align:center
barrier. A following partner read can then use

00:00:32.566 --> 00:00:34.230 position:50% size:92% align:center
the completed shared writes.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:37.356 position:50% size:92% align:center
The barrier does not synchronise an unrelated

00:00:37.356 --> 00:00:39.165 position:50% size:92% align:center
block on this or another SM.

00:00:41.185 --> 00:00:44.117 position:50% size:92% align:center
At partner distance four, threads zero through

00:00:44.117 --> 00:00:46.412 position:50% size:92% align:center
three add the upper four values into

00:00:46.412 --> 00:00:48.006 position:50% size:92% align:center
the lower four positions.

00:00:48.226 --> 00:00:50.954 position:50% size:92% align:center
The next stage must use these completed partial

00:00:50.954 --> 00:00:52.522 position:50% size:92% align:center
sums, so every block thread

00:00:52.522 --> 00:00:54.263 position:50% size:92% align:center
reaches the following barrier.

00:00:56.283 --> 00:00:59.314 position:50% size:92% align:center
The next partner stage leaves two partial sums:

00:00:59.314 --> 00:01:01.958 position:50% size:92% align:center
sixteen point zero and twenty point zero.

00:01:02.178 --> 00:01:05.054 position:50% size:92% align:center
Thread zero adds them to form thirty-six point

00:01:05.054 --> 00:01:07.930 position:50% size:92% align:center
zero. Fewer adding threads does not change the

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:10.306 position:50% size:92% align:center
block’s matching barrier participants.

00:01:12.326 --> 00:01:15.154 position:50% size:92% align:center
Only thread zero performs the final addition.

00:01:15.154 --> 00:01:18.171 position:50% size:92% align:center
None of the eight threads has exited the kernel.

00:01:18.391 --> 00:01:21.002 position:50% size:92% align:center
Pause and predict how many threads must reach

00:01:21.002 --> 00:01:23.033 position:50% size:92% align:center
the matching block barrier. Can the

00:01:23.033 --> 00:01:24.599 position:50% size:92% align:center
other seven simply skip it?

00:01:30.419 --> 00:01:33.104 position:50% size:92% align:center
All eight must reach the matching barrier. The

00:01:33.104 --> 00:01:35.789 position:50% size:92% align:center
arithmetic condition selects who adds; it does

00:01:35.789 --> 00:01:37.423 position:50% size:92% align:center
not remove the other threads

00:01:37.423 --> 00:01:38.590 position:50% size:92% align:center
from the collective.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:41.498 position:50% size:92% align:center
The safe sequence is shared writes, a block

00:01:41.498 --> 00:01:44.499 position:50% size:92% align:center
barrier, then dependent reads. That relationship

00:01:44.499 --> 00:01:46.874 position:50% size:92% align:center
remains true as the reduction narrows.
